WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
Speaker 1: All right. The pollsters really&nbsp;
don't know what's going to happen tonight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:12.420
And I have been a defender of polling to the&nbsp;
extent that we understand the methodology being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:18.600
used. You might remember that the right loves to&nbsp;
say the pollsters got it really wrong in 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:25.500
They really didn't. The national popular vote&nbsp;
polls said it would be a three point margin by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.480
which Hillary would win, and Hillary won by a&nbsp;
little over two points in the national popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:37.320
vote. The polling was correct, but it did not&nbsp;
correctly get the electoral vote because that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:43.140
came down to very small margins in a few states.&nbsp;
So the the general overarching attacks on polling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:49.080
I'm not big on them. They're not super interesting&nbsp;
to me. But it is true that in these particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.700
midterms, the polls may not reflect what's going&nbsp;
to happen tonight. There's a very good Daily Beast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.100
article called Pollsters Have No Effing Idea&nbsp;
What's Going to Happen This Election. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.020
says pollsters and election handicappers are&nbsp;
very worried they could be wrong in decisive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:13.260
ways for the 2022 midterms. Now, this is a this is&nbsp;
a this is a result of two big things. Number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:20.160
the margins, particularly in the Senate, are&nbsp;
expected to be very small. So when you're most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:27.420
likely outcome is one party has 51 seats or the&nbsp;
other party has 51 seats. That's already a range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.780
that only comes down to a couple of seats. Many of&nbsp;
these races are super close because Fetterman is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:41.460
a tie. Walker Warnock is a tie. That's your Senate&nbsp;
right there. That's your, you know, all else being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:47.640
equal. Those two races that are ties determine who&nbsp;
controls the Senate. So because it's very close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:53.160
even a small margin of error can be one party&nbsp;
controls the Senate or another party controls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.680
the Senate. The other thing that's going on is&nbsp;
that there are growing issues and you can read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.740
the article, we'll link to it. There are growing&nbsp;
issues with the polling in two particular ways. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:12.480
lot of the aggregators, like Real Clear Politics,&nbsp;
are using a lot of overtly right wing pollsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:19.380
in their calculations. So just for example,&nbsp;
like if you look at Walker Warnock, you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:26.160
that Trafalgar is being included in the average.&nbsp;
Trafalgar is a known right leaning pollster. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.200
is that throwing off the numbers? That's one&nbsp;
thing. And then number two, to some degree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:38.580
You know, a lot of people don't pick up calls&nbsp;
from unknown numbers at all anymore. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.200
is starting to be a side from the stuff that's&nbsp;
done online for the phone stuff. There's starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.280
to potentially be a sort of self-selection bias&nbsp;
in the people that are interested in getting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.560
the phone and talking to a pollster. And it can&nbsp;
include people who want to sway the polls in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:03:01.080
particular way. Okay. So the point here is it's&nbsp;
Election Day, ignore the polls, but also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.360
polls really may not be telling us what's going&nbsp;
to happen tonight. Now, here's the danger of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:13.260
right wing skew to the polls, if indeed there is&nbsp;
one. You know, we're going to look at clips later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.140
The right is telling us they're going to&nbsp;
claim fraud. They're going to do it. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.480
they're going to claim fraud. And one of the&nbsp;
ways they may claim that fraud is by saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.880
did you see the polls leading up to the election?&nbsp;
You expect us to believe that despite the polls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.600
all these Democrats won. Now, these are the&nbsp;
very same people who in elections past say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.360
you can't trust the polls, but they are going to&nbsp;
weaponize this right wing push polling to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.060
look at the polls and look at what you tell us&nbsp;
was the actual vote. That's not believable. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.820
must have been rigged. That's the danger. We&nbsp;
will have results in a few hours and then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.820 --> 00:04:38.040
can put the polling question aside. Republicans&nbsp;
are already suing to throw out mail in ballots.

